+### X-ray simulation via ray integration
+A rendering technique that simulates X-ray projection by integrating attenuation along viewing rays, accounting for density between source and film plane [S4, unofficial]. The transfer function $\tau(f)$ is set as an identity function if $f$ represents X-ray absorption density from Computed Tomography, and rays are integrated to generate simulated X-ray images from arbitrary directions [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: Not given in the sources. [S1][S2, book][S3, S4, unofficial]
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Not given in the sources. [S1][S2, book][S3, S4, unofficial]
-*   **Strengths**: Effective for visualizing density distributions (e.g., bone in X-rays) or occlusion [S2, book][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Cannot render volumetric effects like fog glow or subsurface scattering; surfaces do not reflect light, only emit [S1][S2, book].
-*   **Compositing**: The compositing equation is commutative, allowing regions to be combined in any order [S2, book].
-*   **When to use**: X-ray simulation, simple occlusion clouds, density distribution visualization [S2, book][S4, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use**: Realistic lighting, shadows, or volumetric illumination effects are required [S1].
